WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.460
I want you to picture three things that, on the surface at least, just seem entirely disconnected

00:07.460 --> 00:13.000
from one another. Okay. So first, a deep sea angelfish, you know, just floating in the absolute

00:13.000 --> 00:19.240
pitch black, crushing depths of the ocean. Right. Second, a completely catastrophic blood-soaked

00:19.240 --> 00:27.300
ancient Roman battlefield from the year 217 BC. Wow, okay. That is quite the jump. Yeah,

00:27.300 --> 00:33.440
just stay with me. And third, that incredibly specific blood-boiling frustration you feel

00:33.440 --> 00:39.420
when a modern smartphone app, you know, it just simply will not let you access or know of the

00:39.420 --> 00:43.980
raw file you just created. I mean, it really sounds like we're setting up a very surreal riddle here.

00:44.200 --> 00:49.320
That is honestly the perfect way to put it, because by the end of this deep dive, you listening to this

00:49.320 --> 00:53.900
are going to see how those three things are just profoundly inextricably linked. It's going to be

00:53.900 --> 00:58.640
a fun one. It is. We have this fascinating stack of research on the table today. We're looking at

00:58.640 --> 01:04.560
three incredibly dense, seemingly unrelated theoretical papers. Yeah, published in 2026.

01:04.840 --> 01:09.800
Right. By an independent researcher who goes by the name Flyxion. And so paper one tackles

01:09.800 --> 01:16.860
evolutionary biology and lower vertebrates. Paper two dives into political science, AI ethics,

01:16.860 --> 01:23.020
algorithmic governance. Which is a heavy one. Very heavy. And then paper three goes straight

01:23.020 --> 01:29.420
into the deep end of theoretical computer science, sheaf theory, and mathematics.

01:29.860 --> 01:32.880
Which on a first pass, you know, you might look at that stack and think,

01:33.040 --> 01:36.180
this researcher is just randomly jumping between academic disciplines.

01:36.300 --> 01:37.640
Yeah, like what is the connection here?

01:37.700 --> 01:43.920
Exactly. But there is a very deliberate muffin here. We're going to unpack the grand unified theory

01:43.920 --> 01:49.760
that connects all three of these papers. And the core theme, like the thread pulling all of this

01:49.760 --> 01:55.860
together, is the concept of hidden constraints. Hidden constraints, yes. We are going to explore

01:55.860 --> 02:00.960
how invisible structures basically dictate the absolute boundaries of what is possible.

02:01.220 --> 02:05.160
Right. And that applies whether we're talking about, you know, the physical tissues and collagen

02:05.160 --> 02:10.200
networks in a biological body. Or the invisible bureaucratic algorithms that are increasingly

02:10.200 --> 02:15.920
governing our society. Exactly. Or even the fundamental software architectures and mathematical

02:15.920 --> 02:20.900
models running our devices. So let's just dive right in. Let's start with the biology piece.

02:21.060 --> 02:25.800
Because I think it grounds this whole idea of constraints beautifully. It's a great starting point, yeah.

02:26.120 --> 02:32.340
Usually when I think about evolution or biology, I think about animals as these modular collections

02:32.340 --> 02:38.920
of traits. Right, like Mr. Potato Head. Yes, exactly. Like a bird evolved wings so it could fly,

02:38.920 --> 02:42.360
a giraffe got a long neck so it could reach the leaves. It feels very additive.

02:43.040 --> 02:46.400
Right. It feels like a checklist of features that natural selection just sort of,

02:46.880 --> 02:50.060
I don't know, tacks onto an animal over millions of years.

02:50.180 --> 02:53.780
But Flyxion seems to completely reject that entire premise.

02:53.920 --> 02:54.760
Totally rejects it.

02:54.840 --> 02:59.120
They do. And they do so by asking us to fundamentally shift our perspective.

02:59.420 --> 02:59.680
Okay.

03:00.060 --> 03:03.420
Instead of looking at an organism as a bundle of accumulated traits,

03:03.420 --> 03:10.040
Flyxion argues we need to look at organisms as solutions within a highly constrained space of possibilities.

03:10.260 --> 03:11.380
A constrained space.

03:11.480 --> 03:17.660
Right. The space of what a biological body can actually be is not an infinite blank canvas.

03:18.060 --> 03:20.700
Natural selection can't just paint whatever it wants.

03:20.860 --> 03:27.040
Exactly. It is strictly bounded by physical laws, by the material properties of tissue,

03:27.040 --> 03:30.780
and by the mechanics of embryonic development.

03:30.780 --> 03:33.760
So an animal isn't just whatever is most fit.

03:34.200 --> 03:38.460
It's whatever the underlying physics of its own body actually allows it to be.

03:38.640 --> 03:39.920
But beautifully, yes.

03:40.160 --> 03:40.420
Okay.

03:40.620 --> 03:46.500
And Flyxion points to one specific material that largely defines the boundaries of that space for vertebrates,

03:46.680 --> 03:48.260
and that is collagen.

03:48.560 --> 03:53.780
Okay. When I hear collagen, my mind immediately goes to like late night skincare infomercials.

03:54.560 --> 03:55.660
Anti-aging creams.

03:55.660 --> 03:57.440
Yeah, joint supplements, that sort of thing.

03:57.580 --> 04:02.080
I do not usually think of it as the invisible architect of biological reality.

04:02.140 --> 04:04.220
Well, that is the popular conception, certainly.

04:04.580 --> 04:07.400
We tend to think of collagen as just biological glue.

04:07.580 --> 04:07.780
Right.

04:07.980 --> 04:12.180
But Flyxion asks us to view collagen as a continuous tensile field.

04:12.340 --> 04:13.040
A tensile field?

04:13.240 --> 04:13.460
Yeah.

04:14.100 --> 04:18.660
It is a three-dimensional mechanical substrate that basically permeates the entire organism.

04:18.900 --> 04:19.200
Oh, yeah.

04:19.300 --> 04:25.220
It forms the network in your connective tissues, your cartilage, your bone matrices, the lining of your blood vessels.

04:25.660 --> 04:26.460
So it's everywhere.

04:26.680 --> 04:27.080
Everywhere.

04:27.700 --> 04:32.660
And this continuous field dictates exactly how tissues resist deformation.

04:33.380 --> 04:36.640
It dictates how forces are transmitted across the whole body.

04:36.720 --> 04:36.940
Okay.

04:37.060 --> 04:39.020
I want to make sure I am visualizing this correctly.

04:39.120 --> 04:39.360
Sir.

04:39.360 --> 04:45.200
Is it sort of like, okay, if you imagine the invisible physics engine inside a video game.

04:45.280 --> 04:46.200
Oh, I like that.

04:46.340 --> 04:46.540
Right.

04:46.860 --> 04:49.860
The physics engine doesn't tell the digital character what to do.

04:50.140 --> 04:51.800
The player makes those choices.

04:52.020 --> 04:56.840
But the physics engine sets the absolute unbreakable rules for the world.

04:56.980 --> 04:57.200
Right.

04:57.480 --> 04:58.780
Gravity, tension.

04:58.780 --> 04:59.420
Exactly.

04:59.600 --> 05:00.720
How objects collide.

05:01.180 --> 05:03.460
How fast something can accelerate before it breaks.

05:03.640 --> 05:03.940
Yes.

05:04.300 --> 05:07.340
So the collagen is essentially biology's physics engine.

05:07.600 --> 05:09.140
I really, really like that analogy.

05:09.340 --> 05:09.580
Yes.

05:10.040 --> 05:13.960
The nervous system might send an electrical signal to a muscle telling it to contract.

05:14.120 --> 05:15.680
That's the player giving a command.

05:15.820 --> 05:16.260
Exactly.

05:16.260 --> 05:33.440
But how that contraction translates into actual physical movement, whether it results in a smooth, fluid, swimming motion through water, or a rigid, articulated step on land, that is entirely dictated by the tensile properties of that surrounding collagen field.

05:33.880 --> 05:35.460
That makes so much sense.

05:35.740 --> 05:39.380
Flyxion calls this the admissible space of an organism's motion.

05:39.900 --> 05:46.140
The collagen field limits the menu of possible actions before the brain even attempts to take an action.

05:46.820 --> 05:47.000
Okay.

05:47.120 --> 05:57.000
So if the underlying material sets the rules for what can evolve, that really changes how we look at animals we typically consider, you know, less evolved.

05:57.160 --> 05:57.840
Oh, absolutely.

05:58.080 --> 06:03.340
Because if the constraint space is the true boss, then there really isn't a ladder of progress, is there?

06:03.540 --> 06:05.320
That is the exact implication here.

06:05.780 --> 06:08.400
Flyxion brings up jawless fish, the agnatha.

06:08.580 --> 06:09.260
Like a lamprey.

06:09.260 --> 06:10.920
Think of a lamprey or hagfish, yeah.

06:11.020 --> 06:11.160
Right.

06:11.160 --> 06:16.860
If you look at them through traditional trait-based biology, you might look at them and say, well, they are missing jaws.

06:16.980 --> 06:18.380
They don't have paired fins.

06:18.640 --> 06:18.900
Right.

06:19.160 --> 06:22.460
They are primitive, incomplete versions of what came later.

06:22.660 --> 06:24.880
Which is incredibly arrogant of us, honestly.

06:25.020 --> 06:25.520
It is.

06:25.620 --> 06:29.320
And mathematically speaking, Flyxion argues it is fundamentally incorrect.

06:29.580 --> 06:30.200
How so?

06:30.360 --> 06:32.660
Jawless fish are not missing anything.

06:32.660 --> 06:40.160
They represent a highly stable, basically perfect solution within their specific region of the constraint space.

06:40.320 --> 06:40.500
Wow.

06:40.640 --> 06:48.160
They utilize a continuous notochord, which is this flexible rod-like structure running down their back.

06:48.220 --> 06:48.380
Right.

06:48.380 --> 06:57.840
In a highly collagenous body without rigid, bony joints, that continuous rod is an absolute masterclass in undulatory swimming.

06:58.020 --> 07:00.840
So when they move, it's just this perfect fluid motion.

07:00.920 --> 07:01.200
Yes.

07:01.700 --> 07:07.620
When their muscles contract, the wave of force passes smoothly down the entire tensile field of their body.

07:07.820 --> 07:09.240
They don't even need rigid joints.

07:09.360 --> 07:09.780
Exactly.

07:10.200 --> 07:17.940
And they feed through suction, which perfectly integrates their respiratory and feeding systems without needing a hinged jaw at all.

07:17.940 --> 07:20.600
But eventually, jaws did evolve invertebrates.

07:20.780 --> 07:21.180
They did.

07:21.460 --> 07:23.420
So how does Flyxion explain that transition?

07:24.240 --> 07:32.440
If the jawless fish was a perfect solution within its constraints, how did biology invent the jaw without breaking the rules of its own physics engine?

07:32.600 --> 07:35.320
Well, biology didn't invent a new material at all.

07:35.400 --> 07:38.360
It didn't suddenly step outside the established constraint space.

07:38.500 --> 07:38.880
They didn't.

07:38.980 --> 07:39.280
No.

07:39.520 --> 07:43.400
It engaged in a radical reorganization of what was already present.

07:43.940 --> 07:44.420
Reorganization?

07:44.500 --> 07:44.800
Yeah.

07:44.800 --> 07:48.620
To understand this, we have to look at something called the pharyngeal arches.

07:49.060 --> 07:50.120
The pharyngeal arches.

07:50.260 --> 07:50.420
Okay.

07:50.520 --> 07:51.840
You're going to have to explain that one to me.

07:52.000 --> 07:52.160
Sure.

07:52.600 --> 08:01.640
So in a jawless fish, as water passes through the gills for breathing, there are structural supports holding those gill slits open.

08:01.760 --> 08:02.500
Like scaffolding.

08:02.760 --> 08:03.240
Exactly.

08:03.540 --> 08:04.840
Those are the pharyngeal arches.

08:05.100 --> 08:08.900
They are essentially static, cartilaginous, or bony scaffolding.

08:08.940 --> 08:09.120
Okay.

08:09.240 --> 08:09.620
I'm with you.

08:09.620 --> 08:17.800
What happened during the evolution of jaws was that the most anterior of these arches, the ones closest to the mouth, simply shifted their geometry.

08:18.320 --> 08:18.980
Wait, really?

08:19.200 --> 08:19.460
Yeah.

08:19.760 --> 08:27.600
Instead of acting as static, passive supports for respiration, their articulation changed, so they began to function as mechanical levers.

08:27.600 --> 08:28.320
Wow.

08:28.920 --> 08:34.680
So evolution didn't just, like, snap a brand new piece onto the organism like a Lego brick.

08:34.840 --> 08:35.340
Not at all.

08:35.620 --> 08:42.120
It took the scaffolding that was holding the breathing tubes open and literally folded it forward to create a hinge.

08:42.580 --> 08:44.620
Reconfiguration within the existing constraints.

08:44.620 --> 08:52.420
The continuous tensile field of the collagen network allowed those formerly static structures to become dynamic levers for capturing prey.

08:52.580 --> 08:54.280
That is just wild to think about.

08:54.400 --> 09:01.620
It is all about how the biological material is organized, rather than constantly adding new, specialized parts from scratch.

09:01.760 --> 09:01.960
Okay.

09:02.080 --> 09:05.660
So we have these organisms operating as highly constrained physical systems.

09:05.960 --> 09:06.180
Hmm.

09:06.620 --> 09:12.280
But a body still has to interact with a dynamic, unpredictable outside world.

09:12.360 --> 09:12.580
Right.

09:12.660 --> 09:13.460
They have to survive.

09:13.460 --> 09:14.100
Exactly.

09:14.300 --> 09:15.180
How do they navigate?

09:15.680 --> 09:24.160
How does an organism with such strict physical limitations find food or avoid danger if it is just a point in a constraint space?

09:24.580 --> 09:30.220
Well, Flyxion argues that we drastically overcomplicate how organisms perceive the world.

09:30.300 --> 09:31.500
Do we think they're smarter than they are?

09:31.660 --> 09:32.300
Essentially, yes.

09:32.420 --> 09:37.000
We assume they need a global, comprehensive map of their environment to survive, but they don't.

09:37.220 --> 09:37.940
So what do they use?

09:38.400 --> 09:42.260
Flyxion points to nematodes' microscopic roundworms.

09:42.460 --> 09:42.760
Okay.

09:42.760 --> 09:46.120
A nematode does not have a spatial map of the soil it lives in.

09:46.280 --> 09:48.820
It operates entirely on what we call temporal sampling.

09:49.020 --> 09:52.460
Meaning it samples its environment over time rather than seeing it all at once?

09:52.620 --> 09:53.100
Exactly.

09:53.580 --> 09:54.660
The nematode moves forward.

09:55.440 --> 10:01.020
If the concentration of a chemical, say, a food source, gets stronger, the nematode just keeps moving forward.

10:01.360 --> 10:06.520
If the chemical concentration begins to weaken, the nematode randomly turns and samples again.

10:06.520 --> 10:10.040
It is simply moving up a chemical gradient.

10:10.040 --> 10:13.200
It's the biological equivalent of playing hot or cold.

10:13.400 --> 10:13.700
Yes.

10:13.860 --> 10:14.500
Moving forward.

10:14.760 --> 10:14.960
Okay.

10:15.100 --> 10:16.260
Getting warmer, getting warmer.

10:16.500 --> 10:17.380
Oh, getting colder.

10:17.720 --> 10:18.660
Let's change direction.

10:18.780 --> 10:21.200
That is the fundamental mechanism of navigation.

10:21.460 --> 10:22.440
Just following the gradient.

10:22.600 --> 10:22.740
Yeah.

10:22.740 --> 10:32.260
And while a nematode is incredibly simple, Flyxion notes that in complex vertebrates, this exact same gradient following principle is preserved.

10:32.480 --> 10:32.760
Really?

10:33.000 --> 10:33.560
Even in us?

10:33.720 --> 10:34.400
Even in us.

10:34.600 --> 10:39.940
It is just amplified and regulated by a neural mechanism known as the seeking system.

10:40.040 --> 10:41.360
The seeking system.

10:41.460 --> 10:42.120
What does that do?

10:42.200 --> 10:44.360
Is that the part of the brain that, like, sets goals?

10:44.360 --> 10:50.860
It is vital to understand that the seeking system does not contain a map and it does not encode specific goals.

10:51.020 --> 10:52.480
It doesn't tell the animal what to look for.

10:52.660 --> 10:52.940
No.

10:53.140 --> 10:53.300
Yeah.

10:53.480 --> 11:01.280
Instead, think of it as a master volume knob or a global gain dial on the animal's sensitivity to environmental gradients.

11:01.280 --> 11:12.660
When the seeking system is highly active, which is fueled largely by dopamine pathways, the organism becomes incredibly sensitive to tiny cues, tiny gradients in the environment.

11:12.900 --> 11:14.280
So it explores, it investigates.

11:14.900 --> 11:16.920
It exhibits high motor activity, exactly.

11:17.100 --> 11:23.480
It basically turns up the contrast on reality so the animal can follow those hot or cold trails more aggressively.

11:23.480 --> 11:32.260
Yes, but Flyxion introduces a brilliant interpretation of what happens when that system is subjected to severe chronic stress.

11:32.460 --> 11:33.840
Oh, this is the anhedonia part.

11:33.980 --> 11:34.200
Yes.

11:34.940 --> 11:41.320
In human psychology, we often talk about anhedonia, the loss of interest, the inability to feel pleasure or motivation.

11:41.600 --> 11:43.580
Right, and we universally pathologize it.

11:43.660 --> 11:44.000
We do.

11:44.140 --> 11:47.220
We view it as a broken brain, a system failure.

11:47.220 --> 11:52.940
Yeah, if you are experiencing anhedonia, the assumption is that something is chemically wrong and needs to be fixed.

11:53.200 --> 11:59.580
But Flyxion frames anhedonia not as a pathology, but as an adaptive habituation to an extreme constraint.

11:59.880 --> 12:01.080
Wait, adaptive?

12:01.260 --> 12:01.620
Yes.

12:01.960 --> 12:09.460
Imagine an organism trapped in an environment that is overwhelmingly stressful, chaotic, or completely devoid of useful information.

12:09.460 --> 12:17.140
If that organism keeps its seeking system cranked up to maximum, it will exhaust its energy reserves responding to noise.

12:17.320 --> 12:20.820
It will physically burn itself out, chasing gradients that lead nowhere.

12:21.120 --> 12:21.680
Precisely.

12:21.940 --> 12:23.360
So what does the system do?

12:23.800 --> 12:25.520
It turns down the gain dial?

12:25.820 --> 12:28.020
It actively desensitizes itself.

12:28.200 --> 12:31.760
It does it to conserve energy and survive the persistent stressor.

12:32.100 --> 12:35.400
That is incredibly profound when you apply it to modern life.

12:35.480 --> 12:35.900
Isn't it?

12:35.900 --> 12:42.880
Think about someone experiencing severe burnout or the exhaustion from, you know, constant doom scrolling on social media.

12:43.020 --> 12:43.480
Oh, yeah.

12:43.680 --> 12:49.780
We are swimming in environments designed to trigger that seeking system constantly with notifications and outrage.

12:49.840 --> 12:51.020
It's an overwhelming gradient.

12:51.500 --> 12:51.740
Right.

12:52.400 --> 12:54.760
Maybe feeling numb isn't our brains breaking.

12:55.120 --> 13:02.720
Maybe it is our underlying biological constraint system aggressively turning down the volume to protect us from an uninformative, hostile environment.

13:03.040 --> 13:04.300
It is a protective constraint.

13:04.440 --> 13:04.900
Exactly.

13:05.300 --> 13:05.620
Wow.

13:05.900 --> 13:15.100
But if we want to examine what happens when biological constraints are pushed to the absolute razor-edge limits of survival, Flyxion points us toward the deep-sea anglerfish.

13:15.500 --> 13:15.840
Yes.

13:16.460 --> 13:18.280
This part of the paper is wild.

13:18.760 --> 13:20.880
The deep-sea is basically an alien world.

13:21.120 --> 13:22.080
It really is.

13:22.180 --> 13:26.900
It is one of the most severe ecological constraint spaces on the entire planet.

13:26.960 --> 13:27.400
It's black.

13:27.520 --> 13:28.040
Pitch black.

13:28.040 --> 13:33.100
The water pressure is immense, and biomass is incredibly sparse.

13:33.280 --> 13:33.860
It's a desert.

13:34.100 --> 13:34.520
It is.

13:34.520 --> 13:46.900
It's an ecological bottleneck.

13:46.900 --> 13:47.320
Yes.

13:47.320 --> 13:51.900
So when he finally does find her against all odds, he has to make sure he never loses her again.

13:51.980 --> 13:52.160
Right.

13:52.420 --> 13:55.860
And to solve this problem, he physically bites onto her side.

13:55.940 --> 13:56.820
He bites onto her.

13:56.820 --> 14:00.820
And what happens next is just a fundamental breakdown of biological boundaries.

14:00.820 --> 14:01.400
It's incredible.

14:01.400 --> 14:06.020
The male's mouth and the female's skin begin to physically degrade at the point of contact.

14:06.020 --> 14:06.820
They dissolve.

14:06.820 --> 14:07.400
They do.

14:07.400 --> 14:09.060
Their underlying tissues merge.

14:09.060 --> 14:13.080
Their vascular systems connect, meaning they begin to share a single blood supply.

14:13.080 --> 14:13.580
Oh, wow.

14:13.580 --> 14:22.440
And returning to our earlier concept, their collagen networks reorganize to form a continuous structural bond between the two previously separate bodies.

14:22.440 --> 14:25.400
So the male literally dissolves his own independence.

14:25.400 --> 14:25.960
It does.

14:25.960 --> 14:29.800
He becomes a permanent, physically attached reproductive organ on the female's body.

14:29.800 --> 14:30.180
Yes.

14:30.360 --> 14:32.720
But wait, how does the biology actually allow that?

14:33.140 --> 14:41.800
Like, if a human gets a kidney transplant from someone who isn't a perfect match, our immune system goes to war to destroy the foreign tissue.

14:41.880 --> 14:42.100
Right.

14:42.240 --> 14:42.880
Rejection.

14:42.880 --> 14:43.360
Yeah.

14:43.760 --> 14:51.560
How do two separate fish just merge bloodstreams without their immune systems tearing each other apart?

14:52.260 --> 14:53.780
That is the central mystery.

14:54.040 --> 14:58.880
And Rejection addresses it by looking at how extreme external constraints force internal compromises.

14:59.640 --> 14:59.800
Okay.

15:00.060 --> 15:00.580
I'll back that.

15:00.700 --> 15:10.060
In some species of anglofish, the genes that encode the core components of the adaptive immune system, the mechanisms that recognize and attack foreign tissue,

15:10.060 --> 15:13.060
they're radically modified or functionally absent entirely.

15:13.860 --> 15:14.720
Well, they just don't have them.

15:15.340 --> 15:27.240
The ecological pressure to reproduce in a sparse environment is so severe that maintaining a robust histocompatibility barrier maintaining strict biological individuality becomes a lethal luxury.

15:27.400 --> 15:28.320
A lethal luxury.

15:28.440 --> 15:28.700
Wow.

15:28.960 --> 15:32.940
The constraint of the environment overrides the constraint of the immune system.

15:33.160 --> 15:37.060
So the two individuals become a single composite constraint system.

15:37.340 --> 15:37.820
Exactly.

15:37.820 --> 15:42.960
The female handles feeding and locomotion, and the male handles reproduction.

15:43.480 --> 15:48.540
Individuality itself is a biological luxury that gets abandoned if the gradient is harsh enough.

15:48.640 --> 15:49.080
Exactly.

15:49.400 --> 15:50.040
That is haunting.

15:50.480 --> 15:56.240
So organisms are constantly navigating these physical, chemical, and ecological gradients just to survive.

15:56.420 --> 15:56.800
Yes.

15:57.000 --> 15:59.640
But I want to follow that thread into Flyxion's second paper.

15:59.720 --> 16:00.280
Let's do it.

16:00.280 --> 16:09.560
Because if physical environments force organisms to fuse or, you know, shut down their seeking systems, what happens when the environment isn't physical?

16:09.820 --> 16:10.060
Right.

16:10.060 --> 16:17.060
What happens when the gradients we have to navigate to survive are created by human society, by bureaucracy?

16:17.060 --> 16:22.160
This is where Flyxion shifts from biological architecture to socio-political architecture.

16:22.320 --> 16:22.560
Okay.

16:23.040 --> 16:28.760
In the quiet battlefield, the focus turns to the ethics of artificial intelligence and algorithmic governance.

16:29.280 --> 16:31.400
Now, I need to jump in here for a second.

16:32.280 --> 16:42.080
Flyxion wades into some heavily debated, highly charged political territory here regarding policing, government welfare programs, and social control.

16:42.080 --> 16:42.960
Yes, they do.

16:43.660 --> 16:51.480
Just to be entirely clear for you listening, we are not here to endorse or debate the specific political ideology Flyxion holds.

16:51.620 --> 16:52.520
Absolutely not.

16:52.820 --> 17:03.100
But we absolutely need to look objectively at the underlying architecture they describe, because it is vital to understanding this grand, unified theory of constraints.

17:03.340 --> 17:03.760
Agreed.

17:03.840 --> 17:05.860
We are analyzing the structural argument.

17:06.120 --> 17:06.300
Right.

17:06.380 --> 17:11.000
And Flyxion begins this paper with a scathing critique of the current state of AI ethics.

17:11.000 --> 17:11.280
Okay.

17:11.440 --> 17:12.000
What's the critique?

17:12.520 --> 17:17.060
They argue that both public anxiety and academic focus are radically misdirected.

17:17.400 --> 17:18.100
Misdirected how?

17:18.280 --> 17:22.560
The entire conversation is obsessively fixated on the concept of the autonomous weapon.

17:22.680 --> 17:23.160
Oh, right.

17:23.280 --> 17:24.000
The killer drone.

17:24.340 --> 17:25.360
The Terminator scenario.

17:25.460 --> 17:25.860
Exactly.

17:26.040 --> 17:30.340
The AI that decides, all on its own, to target a human and pull the trigger.

17:30.440 --> 17:30.600
Yeah.

17:30.840 --> 17:32.460
Why do we focus on that so much?

17:32.500 --> 17:34.560
Is it just because Hollywood trained us to fear it?

17:34.680 --> 17:39.100
Hollywood definitely plays a role, but Flyxion argues it is deeper than that.

17:39.100 --> 17:42.480
We focus on the killer drone because it is highly legible.

17:42.680 --> 17:42.980
Legible.

17:43.180 --> 17:47.640
It fits neatly into our traditional historical understanding of what a weapon is.

17:47.700 --> 17:47.860
Right.

17:47.920 --> 17:48.380
It goes boom.

17:48.660 --> 17:49.080
Exactly.

17:49.080 --> 17:55.120
But Flyxion claims that by staring obsessively at the sky, waiting for the spectacular robot army,

17:55.120 --> 18:01.200
we are entirely ignoring the devastating harm that AI systems are already inflicting on the ground right now.

18:01.200 --> 18:03.440
Okay, so how do they illustrate this misdirection?

18:03.760 --> 18:10.280
They draw this magnificent historical parallel to the year 217 B.C. in ancient Rome.

18:10.300 --> 18:11.880
But I love a good Roman history detour.

18:12.000 --> 18:12.860
Set the stage for us.

18:13.200 --> 18:15.320
What has happened in 217 B.C.?

18:15.320 --> 18:17.220
Rome is in an existential crisis.

18:17.640 --> 18:25.120
The Carthaginian general Hannibal has crossed the Alps and just inflicted two utterly catastrophic, humiliating defeats on the Roman legions.

18:25.300 --> 18:26.580
Okay, so things are bad.

18:26.720 --> 18:27.300
Very bad.

18:27.300 --> 18:33.800
The Roman Senate is terrified, so they invoke emergency powers and appoint a dictator named Quintus Fabius Maximus.

18:33.840 --> 18:35.600
And his job is basically to save the republic.

18:35.840 --> 18:40.520
His single overriding mandate is to save the Roman Republic from annihilation.

18:40.660 --> 18:41.140
No pressure.

18:41.360 --> 18:41.580
Right.

18:41.900 --> 18:48.140
Now, the Roman public and the political elite expected Fabius to do what Roman generals basically always did.

18:48.220 --> 18:48.460
Right.

18:48.460 --> 18:58.460
They demanded that he march the legions out, you know, meet Hannibal on an open field, clash swords, and win this spectacular, glorious victory.

18:58.600 --> 18:59.980
They wanted a cinematic showdown.

19:00.340 --> 19:03.860
They wanted the clear, undeniable clash of good versus evil.

19:04.040 --> 19:04.280
Yes.

19:05.000 --> 19:09.760
But Fabius was a brilliant strategist, and he recognized a hidden constraint here.

19:10.000 --> 19:10.160
Yeah.

19:10.600 --> 19:16.340
Hannibal's army was incredibly lethal in a pitched battle, but they were deeply vulnerable in another way.

19:16.420 --> 19:16.920
Oh, so.

19:16.920 --> 19:24.500
They were hundreds of miles from home, operating in enemy territory, and entirely dependent on foraging for food and supplies to survive.

19:24.720 --> 19:25.440
Ah, logistics.

19:25.700 --> 19:26.220
Logistics.

19:26.680 --> 19:30.500
So Fabius completely refused to give the public their spectacular battle.

19:30.720 --> 19:31.500
What did he do instead?

19:32.140 --> 19:35.140
He kept his legions up in the hills, just shadowing Hannibal.

19:35.460 --> 19:39.340
He ordered the Roman farmers to burn their own crops and abandon their villages.

19:39.720 --> 19:40.480
Scourged earth.

19:40.620 --> 19:44.300
He engaged in constant low-level harassment of Hannibal's foraging parties.

19:44.300 --> 19:49.780
He fought a slow, quiet, agonizing war of logistics, starvation, and attrition.

19:50.240 --> 19:52.740
And how did the Roman public react to that?

19:53.180 --> 19:54.400
They despised him for it.

19:54.500 --> 19:54.780
Really?

19:55.000 --> 19:55.420
Oh, yeah.

19:55.880 --> 19:56.900
They thought he was a coward.

19:57.420 --> 20:01.200
They mocked him with the title cunctator, which means the delayer.

20:01.400 --> 20:01.980
Oh, wow.

20:01.980 --> 20:07.700
The political pressure grew so intense that eventually Rome essentially sidelined Fabius.

20:08.180 --> 20:18.140
They elected new consuls, specifically one named Varro, who promised to abandon the delay tactics and give the public the spectacular, blood-soaked battle they craved.

20:18.440 --> 20:20.680
And how did that work out for them?

20:20.680 --> 20:27.520
It resulted in the Battle of Cannae, which remains, to this day, one of the most horrific military slaughters in human history.

20:27.700 --> 20:28.440
Oh, my God.

20:28.480 --> 20:35.200
Hannibal encircled the massive Roman army and annihilated tens of thousands of Roman citizens in a single afternoon.

20:35.460 --> 20:35.820
Wow.

20:36.120 --> 20:41.780
The demand for spectacular violence basically blinded them to the effectiveness of logistical warfare.

20:42.040 --> 20:43.760
Okay, that is an incredible piece of history.

20:43.960 --> 20:44.100
Yeah.

20:44.100 --> 20:50.300
But how exactly is Flyxion connecting a Roman military disaster to modern artificial intelligence?

20:50.620 --> 20:55.020
Flyxion uses this historical event to define two distinct categories of violence.

20:55.400 --> 20:57.880
The first is spectacular violence.

20:58.500 --> 21:00.120
That is the Battle of Cannae.

21:00.740 --> 21:02.480
It is a kinetic drone strike.

21:02.620 --> 21:03.260
It is a bombing.

21:03.540 --> 21:03.620
Right.

21:03.840 --> 21:07.200
Spectacular violence is kinetically concentrated in time and space.

21:07.420 --> 21:08.340
It is embodied.

21:08.760 --> 21:11.560
And most importantly, it is universally recognized.

21:11.560 --> 21:15.980
When a drone fires a missile, no one debates whether or not violence just occurred.

21:16.780 --> 21:19.180
The second category is administrative violence.

21:19.520 --> 21:20.480
Administrative violence.

21:20.580 --> 21:23.440
This is what Fabius was doing by burning the supply lines.

21:23.640 --> 21:24.640
It is diffuse.

21:25.220 --> 21:26.560
It is bureaucratic.

21:26.900 --> 21:30.360
It is stretched out over long periods of time.

21:30.600 --> 21:30.860
Okay.

21:30.960 --> 21:35.760
It targets the underlying logistics of survival rather than the physical body itself.

21:35.920 --> 21:36.160
Right.

21:36.540 --> 21:41.540
Flyxion argues that while we are busy writing ethics papers about the AI equivalent of the Battle of Cannae,

21:41.560 --> 21:49.520
you know, the killer drone, we are currently being subjected to massive widespread campaigns of AI-driven administrative violence.

21:49.720 --> 21:53.540
I hear the argument, but I want to stop and really challenge the terminology here.

21:53.660 --> 21:54.120
Fair enough.

21:54.360 --> 21:59.380
Calling a bureaucratic process violence feels like a massive stretch to me.

21:59.740 --> 22:04.800
I mean, if the DMV is slow in processing my license renewal, it's incredibly annoying.

22:05.380 --> 22:06.420
But it isn't violence.

22:06.420 --> 22:10.440
Aren't we diluting the meaning of the word by applying it to algorithms?

22:10.740 --> 22:13.700
That is the standard counter-argument, and it's a valid question.

22:14.200 --> 22:20.240
But Flyxion defends the term by focusing on the ultimate outcome, which is survival.

22:20.460 --> 22:21.020
Survival.

22:21.180 --> 22:24.060
What are these algorithmic systems actually managing today?

22:24.180 --> 22:25.960
They are not just running DMV lines.

22:26.040 --> 22:26.720
What are they doing?

22:26.720 --> 22:33.580
Local and federal governments are increasingly deploying opaque algorithms to determine who is eligible for public housing.

22:33.720 --> 22:40.020
They are using AI to detect supposed fraud and automatically revoke food stamps or Medicaid benefits.

22:40.180 --> 22:40.520
Oh.

22:40.680 --> 22:48.680
They are using risk assessment algorithms in the criminal justice system to recommend who gets released on bail and who remains locked in a cage before trial.

22:48.800 --> 22:49.540
Oh, I see.

22:49.540 --> 22:56.680
And they are using predictive models in child welfare departments to flag which families should be investigated for child removal.

22:57.020 --> 23:00.720
So they are managing the actual resources required to stay alive and stay free.

23:01.120 --> 23:01.640
Precisely.

23:01.840 --> 23:02.320
Excuse me.

23:02.420 --> 23:04.520
I mean, that is the exact point Flyxion is making.

23:04.940 --> 23:05.020
Yeah.

23:05.160 --> 23:13.780
If an automated system erroneously revokes your food subsidies or your medical coverage, it is directly threatening your physical survival.

23:14.040 --> 23:14.420
It is.

23:14.420 --> 23:19.980
If an algorithm flags your family, resulting in the state taking your children, that is a profound trauma.

23:20.200 --> 23:20.400
Right.

23:20.720 --> 23:30.580
But because this harm is executed via a denied application form or a risk score on a caseworker's dashboard rather than, say, a kinetic explosion.

23:30.780 --> 23:32.360
We do not recognize it as a weapon.

23:32.620 --> 23:33.020
Exactly.

23:33.420 --> 23:34.980
The bureaucracy obscures the harm.

23:35.280 --> 23:38.680
We fail to see that starving of the saw line is just as lethal as the sword.

23:38.680 --> 23:43.620
We are so focused on looking for the smoke and the fire that we don't realize the water has been shut off.

23:43.620 --> 23:45.000
Beautifully put, yes.

23:45.280 --> 23:45.740
But wait.

23:46.360 --> 23:54.060
Whenever these civil AI systems are deployed, the companies selling them always assure the public that the AI isn't making the final call.

23:54.160 --> 23:54.340
Right.

23:54.420 --> 23:55.500
The classic defense.

23:55.680 --> 23:55.820
Yeah.

23:55.940 --> 23:57.480
There is always a human in the loop.

23:57.800 --> 23:59.860
A judge reviews the bail recommendation.

24:00.600 --> 24:02.520
A police worker reviews the welfare flag.

24:03.120 --> 24:07.220
Doesn't that human oversight act as a safeguard against this administrative violence?

24:07.660 --> 24:13.480
Flyxion attacks that defense directly by introducing a concept called the locus of decision logic.

24:13.620 --> 24:15.140
The locus of decision logic, okay.

24:15.700 --> 24:25.340
Their argument is that simply inserting a human somewhere into the bureaucratic loop is morally meaningless if that human does not possess true agency.

24:25.520 --> 24:26.060
Meaningless.

24:26.640 --> 24:27.040
How?

24:27.380 --> 24:30.480
Consider the structural constraints placed on that human.

24:31.360 --> 24:34.100
Let's say you are a child welfare caseworker.

24:34.260 --> 24:34.440
Okay.

24:34.440 --> 24:38.920
You are overworked, you're underpaid, and you're basically drowning in cases.

24:39.060 --> 24:39.760
Typical scenario.

24:39.960 --> 24:50.600
Your dashboard flashes a red alert because an opaque algorithm, a black box system whose mathematical weights you're not allowed to see, has determined that a specific family is high risk.

24:50.840 --> 24:51.120
Right.

24:51.120 --> 24:57.240
If you decide to override the machine and close the case, and God forbid, something tragic happens to that child later.

24:57.280 --> 24:57.700
Oh, I see.

24:57.820 --> 25:00.540
You are entirely legally and professionally liable.

25:00.680 --> 25:01.200
You are.

25:01.360 --> 25:03.080
You ignored the computer's warning.

25:03.420 --> 25:09.880
However, if you follow the computer's recommendation and intervene, even if it tears an innocent family apart, you are protected.

25:10.140 --> 25:11.660
Because you were just following the data.

25:11.840 --> 25:12.020
Exactly.

25:12.020 --> 25:12.320
Uh-huh.

25:12.320 --> 25:15.780
So the human isn't an actual decision maker.

25:16.420 --> 25:19.740
The human is just a liability shield for the algorithm.

25:20.180 --> 25:20.900
Exactly that.

25:21.560 --> 25:28.260
For a human in the loop to matter, Flyxion argues, the human must be positioned causally upstream of the decision.

25:28.600 --> 25:29.080
Upstream.

25:29.080 --> 25:38.500
They must have a transparent understanding of how the algorithm reached its conclusion, and they must have the structural authority to alter the outcome without risking their livelihood.

25:38.740 --> 25:39.700
Which they don't have.

25:39.980 --> 25:41.560
Under current implementations, no.

25:41.560 --> 25:48.340
The AI holds the true locus of decision logic, while the human merely rubber stamps the administrative violence.

25:48.660 --> 25:50.740
We are being governed by hidden constraints.

25:51.580 --> 25:59.300
The algorithm creates a gradient of survival, and families are just forced to navigate it, much like an organism navigating a harsh environment.

25:59.400 --> 26:00.740
It's the exact same dynamic.

26:00.960 --> 26:02.800
But this raises a massive question.

26:03.420 --> 26:09.640
If we are handing over the locus of decision logic to these machines, how do the machines actually think?

26:09.940 --> 26:10.240
Right.

26:10.240 --> 26:15.700
Why do they make these catastrophic errors where they deny someone's benefits for seemingly no logical reason?

26:15.980 --> 26:19.640
And that question bridges us perfectly into Flyxion's third paper.

26:19.860 --> 26:21.200
From Accumulation to Closure.

26:21.620 --> 26:22.020
Yes.

26:22.280 --> 26:27.420
Where we actually open up the hood of the AI to examine its mathematical architecture.

26:27.420 --> 26:30.560
Okay, I want to give everyone listening a slight heads up here.

26:30.980 --> 26:35.400
We are about to wade into the theoretical computer science weeds.

26:35.400 --> 26:36.580
It gets dense.

26:37.020 --> 26:37.160
Yeah.

26:37.300 --> 26:41.960
Flyxion leans heavily into sheaf theory, inverse problems, and topology.

26:42.520 --> 26:44.900
But stay with us, because we are going to break this down.

26:45.300 --> 26:48.280
The underlying concepts are actually deeply intuitive.

26:48.280 --> 26:49.020
They are.

26:49.300 --> 26:57.860
To understand why these systems fail in such bizarre ways, Flyxion says we have to understand the fundamental difference between prediction and realizability.

26:58.080 --> 26:59.420
Prediction and realizability.

26:59.680 --> 27:00.880
Okay, let's start with prediction.

27:01.520 --> 27:05.300
Modern large language models, at their core, are predictive engines.

27:05.840 --> 27:10.660
They ingest massive amounts of text and learn the statistical relationships between words.

27:10.800 --> 27:10.980
Right.

27:10.980 --> 27:16.100
When you ask them a question, they calculate the probability of what the next word should be, and then the next, and the next.

27:16.260 --> 27:20.040
They are incredibly good at producing text that is locally plausible.

27:20.180 --> 27:21.140
Yes, exactly.

27:21.340 --> 27:22.820
They sound very, very confident.

27:23.000 --> 27:24.140
But what is realizability?

27:24.960 --> 27:34.980
Realizability, or what Flyxion also calls constraint closure, is the requirement that a set of statements actually belongs to a single, consistent, underlying reality.

27:35.740 --> 27:37.900
Okay, and modern AI systems don't do that.

27:37.960 --> 27:38.160
No.

27:38.160 --> 27:40.360
They do not enforce this constraint.

27:40.360 --> 27:45.720
They do not maintain an internal world model that they check their predictions against.

27:45.980 --> 27:46.520
So what happens?

27:46.800 --> 27:56.520
Because of this, an AI can generate two statements that are each individually highly probable based on the training data, but which are mutually impossible when placed next to each other.

27:56.640 --> 27:57.160
Ah, okay.

27:57.360 --> 28:00.640
Think of it like interrogating a highly articulate liar.

28:00.920 --> 28:01.760
I love this analogy.

28:01.900 --> 28:02.140
Go ahead.

28:02.140 --> 28:08.980
So imagine you meet someone who is incredibly charismatic and quick on their feet, but they are a compulsive liar.

28:09.140 --> 28:09.420
Right.

28:09.420 --> 28:16.180
You ask them what they did yesterday, and they spin a beautiful, vivid story about having breakfast at a cafe in Paris.

28:16.740 --> 28:18.080
It sounds completely realistic.

28:18.280 --> 28:18.560
Mm-hmm.

28:19.180 --> 28:26.940
Later, someone else asks them about their morning, and they tell an equally vivid, detailed story about hiking up a snowy trail in the Swiss Alps.

28:26.980 --> 28:27.240
Mm-hmm.

28:27.240 --> 28:29.260
Both of those stories are locally plausible.

28:30.260 --> 28:35.520
But if you zoom out and look at the whole picture, the timeline is physically impossible.

28:35.520 --> 28:37.520
You cannot be in two places once.

28:38.220 --> 28:38.580
Exactly.

28:39.120 --> 28:43.640
You cannot be drinking coffee in Paris and hiking a mountain in Switzerland at the exact same moment.

28:44.160 --> 28:49.440
The liar gets caught because they don't have an actual historical world model in their head.

28:49.660 --> 28:52.620
They're just generating plausible-sounding sentences in the moment.

28:52.880 --> 28:59.780
That is a brilliant way to conceptualize what Flyxion describes mathematically as the failure of the fiber product.

29:00.000 --> 29:00.780
The fiber product.

29:00.780 --> 29:11.680
Yes. The AI gathers observations that align with localized patterns in its data, but when you try to find the intersection of all those statements, the fiber product, the set, is empty.

29:11.860 --> 29:14.400
Meaning there's no underlying reality to support it.

29:14.560 --> 29:20.200
Exactly. There is no actual state of the world that could produce all of those outputs simultaneously.

29:20.800 --> 29:24.240
This is what we colloquially call an AI hallucination.

29:24.420 --> 29:26.300
Right, when the AI just makes stuff up.

29:26.300 --> 29:30.460
Yes, and Flyxion insists we stop treating hallucinations as software bugs.

29:30.780 --> 29:31.520
They aren't bugs.

29:31.860 --> 29:32.780
They are not bugs.

29:33.400 --> 29:38.980
They are the structural inevitability of a system that predicts without enforcing realizability.

29:39.480 --> 29:42.420
But wait. AI developers know this is a problem, right?

29:43.100 --> 29:45.200
And they've introduced techniques to try and fix it.

29:45.360 --> 29:45.880
They have.

29:46.040 --> 29:48.400
The most famous one is chain of thought prompting.

29:48.400 --> 29:56.640
If you tell the AI to think step by step, it outputs its reasoning process before giving you the final answer.

29:57.120 --> 29:59.720
Doesn't that force it to maintain a consistent world model?

29:59.720 --> 30:03.400
Flyxion is incredibly critical of chain of thought prompting.

30:03.520 --> 30:04.060
Oh, really?

30:04.160 --> 30:09.420
Yes. They call it an illusion of reasoning, specifically labeling it post-hoc narration.

30:09.680 --> 30:10.600
Post-hoc narration.

30:10.800 --> 30:11.080
Mm-hmm.

30:11.160 --> 30:15.480
So you're saying the AI is just making up the explanation after it has already decided on the answer.

30:15.640 --> 30:16.940
Let's look at the mechanics of it.

30:17.480 --> 30:24.360
When a human solves a complex math problem on a whiteboard, the steps they write down are causally driving them toward the conclusion.

30:24.360 --> 30:26.260
The reasoning produces the answer.

30:26.480 --> 30:26.880
Exactly.

30:27.300 --> 30:37.200
But with an LLM using chain of thought, research suggests the visible text it generates on your screen, the reasoning, is often not causally upstream of the final output.

30:37.420 --> 30:39.000
It's not driving the conclusion.

30:39.380 --> 30:39.720
No.

30:40.400 --> 30:52.800
In experimental settings, researchers have deliberately corrupted or altered the intermediate reasoning steps the AI prints out, and the AI often still arrives at the exact same final conclusion regardless.

30:52.800 --> 30:53.480
Oh, wow.

30:53.860 --> 30:58.540
The narrative you are reading is just a shadow cast by the underlying computation.

30:59.080 --> 31:01.280
It is not the engine driving the train.

31:01.540 --> 31:08.180
So it's essentially just generating text that looks like human reasoning to satisfy our desire to see it work.

31:08.360 --> 31:08.580
Yeah.

31:08.680 --> 31:11.580
But the actual computation is happening somewhere entirely different.

31:11.720 --> 31:12.040
Yes.

31:12.160 --> 31:13.360
Okay. So how do we fix it?

31:13.360 --> 31:18.580
To truly solve this, Flyxion proposes a shift from chain of thought to chain of memory.

31:18.900 --> 31:19.480
Chain of memory.

31:19.520 --> 31:19.840
Yes.

31:19.840 --> 31:27.840
Instead of forcing the AI to generate a string of text tokens to represent reasoning, the system needs to maintain a hidden latent state trajectory.

31:28.580 --> 31:28.760
Okay.

31:29.100 --> 31:37.300
The reasoning process should be a sequence of mathematical state transformations occurring within the AI's memory, which then causally determine the final output.

31:37.300 --> 31:40.220
I see where you're going, but I have to play devil's advocate for a second here.

31:40.280 --> 31:40.620
Please do.

31:40.740 --> 31:45.500
If I boot up an LLM right now and ask it to play a game of chess with me, it can do it.

31:45.720 --> 31:46.000
Right.

31:46.120 --> 31:48.160
It knows where the pieces are.

31:48.260 --> 31:50.560
It doesn't try to move a knight like a bishop.

31:51.280 --> 31:55.320
Doesn't that prove it has some kind of internal world model of the chessboard?

31:55.500 --> 32:01.880
It is a great counter example, but it actually proves Flyxion's deeper point about accumulation versus closure.

32:02.280 --> 32:02.620
Okay.

32:02.920 --> 32:05.560
Which brings us to the Sheaf theoretic diagnosis.

32:05.560 --> 32:06.460
All right. Hit me.

32:06.460 --> 32:13.940
When an LLM plays chess, it is largely relying on having memorized millions of recorded chess games.

32:14.180 --> 32:15.860
It is accumulating patterns.

32:16.180 --> 32:16.400
Right.

32:16.600 --> 32:26.920
But if you ask an LLM to play chess on a board that is 10 by 10 instead of 8 by 8, or you invent a completely new piece with new rules, it often fails catastrophically.

32:27.020 --> 32:29.000
Because it hasn't memorized that exact board before.

32:29.120 --> 32:29.500
Exactly.

32:29.660 --> 32:32.780
It cannot generalize the rules of space because it never learned the rules.

32:32.780 --> 32:36.220
It only accumulated the statistical history of the standard game.

32:36.460 --> 32:36.960
Ah.

32:36.960 --> 32:42.180
To explain why accumulation fails to produce true knowledge, Flyxion uses Sheaf theory.

32:42.180 --> 32:44.440
And again, we can conceptualize this without the heavy math.

32:44.440 --> 32:45.440
Let's try.

32:45.440 --> 32:49.720
In Sheaf theory, you deal with local sections and global gluing.

32:49.720 --> 32:50.920
Okay. Local sections.

32:50.920 --> 32:54.680
Imagine an AI system designed to act as an all-knowing database.

32:55.140 --> 32:57.640
It constantly ingests documents from the internet.

32:58.200 --> 33:01.060
Each individual document is a local section.

33:01.980 --> 33:05.220
Within its own specific context, the document makes sense.

33:05.480 --> 33:08.040
Like a Wikipedia article about a historical event.

33:08.140 --> 33:08.560
Exactly.

33:08.560 --> 33:18.020
Now, what happens if the system ingests one article that claims an event happened in 1990 and then ingests another article that claims the exact same event happened in 1995?

33:18.480 --> 33:19.000
A contradiction.

33:19.440 --> 33:19.660
Right.

33:19.780 --> 33:22.680
An accumulation-based system simply stores both.

33:22.820 --> 33:25.580
It adds both facts to its massive pile of parameters.

33:25.640 --> 33:26.760
It just hoards the data.

33:26.760 --> 33:33.660
Yes. It might note that they are associated with the same topic, but it lacks the mechanism for global gluing.

33:33.980 --> 33:34.400
Which is?

33:34.620 --> 33:40.120
The ability to stitch those local sections into a single, globally consistent tapestry.

33:40.360 --> 33:42.020
It tolerates the contradiction.

33:42.240 --> 33:42.580
Wow.

33:42.580 --> 33:50.620
Flyxion argues that true epistemic progress, the actual process of gaining knowledge and reasoning, is not an additive process.

33:50.920 --> 33:51.920
It is subtractive.

33:52.000 --> 33:53.320
Wait. Learning is subtractive.

33:53.580 --> 33:56.900
It is about contracting the feasible set of what is possible.

33:56.900 --> 34:04.040
When you encounter two contradictory facts, true reasoning requires you to use constraints to rule one of them out.

34:04.340 --> 34:07.500
Or to realize that neither can be true, given a third fact you know.

34:07.720 --> 34:08.000
Right.

34:08.000 --> 34:18.720
Every new piece of verified information should act as a blade, slicing away impossible world states, until only one consistent, non-contradictory reality remains.

34:19.280 --> 34:21.140
That is constraint closure.

34:21.660 --> 34:26.180
So building an AI isn't about stuffing more and more data into a massive filing cabinet.

34:26.540 --> 34:27.660
It's more like sculpting.

34:27.740 --> 34:28.820
It's a sculpting, yes.

34:28.960 --> 34:32.060
You start with a block of marble, which is all the possibilities in the universe.

34:32.060 --> 34:38.440
And every true fact chips away a piece of marble until you reveal the single, coherent sculpture inside.

34:38.560 --> 34:39.300
A beautiful metaphor.

34:39.820 --> 34:45.760
And the tragedy, according to Flyxion, is that the entire AI industry is basically measuring the wrong thing.

34:45.980 --> 34:46.420
How so?

34:46.920 --> 34:49.560
Standard benchmarks test for marginal accuracy.

34:49.860 --> 34:53.520
Can the AI answer this one specific trivia question correctly in isolation?

34:53.880 --> 35:00.300
Those benchmarks are completely blind to whether the AI's internal worldview is mathematically coherent.

35:00.300 --> 35:04.580
Flyxion insists we need to start measuring the realizability rate.

35:04.720 --> 35:05.780
For realizability rate.

35:05.840 --> 35:12.800
We need to test how often an AI's total set of generated answers could actually coexist without breaking the laws of logic or physics.

35:12.940 --> 35:18.960
This all builds to what might be the most relatable and honestly the most frustrating conclusion in the entire paper.

35:19.120 --> 35:20.540
I know exactly where you were going.

35:20.980 --> 35:28.020
Flyxion connects this deep mathematical failure of AI directly to the everyday software we use on our phones and computers.

35:28.020 --> 35:34.140
They refer to a phenomenon we've all experienced as the inshittification of state.

35:34.360 --> 35:34.600
Yes.

35:35.340 --> 35:38.320
Flyxion draws a profound structural parallel here.

35:38.400 --> 35:39.200
Let's unpack that.

35:39.480 --> 35:50.360
Just as these AI systems lack access to a true underlying world state, modern technology platforms are deliberately designed to hide the underlying computational state from you, the user.

35:50.700 --> 35:54.640
Anyone who used computers in the 90s or early 2000s knows exactly what this means.

35:54.700 --> 35:55.260
Oh, absolutely.

35:55.260 --> 35:57.000
Think about the old Unix philosophy.

35:57.380 --> 35:58.620
You had files.

35:59.540 --> 36:00.420
You had directories.

36:00.760 --> 36:04.960
If you created a text document, it was a .txt file sitting right there in a folder.

36:05.100 --> 36:05.340
Right.

36:05.600 --> 36:07.180
You had raw access to the data.

36:07.460 --> 36:14.680
You could open it in one program, modify it, save it, and then drag that exact same file into a completely different program.

36:14.900 --> 36:17.900
You had absolute visibility into the state of the machine.

36:18.100 --> 36:19.200
And look at what we have now.

36:19.520 --> 36:24.220
Everything is locked behind these glossy, opaque, rounded corner app interfaces.

36:24.220 --> 36:25.700
It's totally walled off.

36:25.900 --> 36:26.240
Exactly.

36:26.720 --> 36:31.340
If I take a photo using a specific social media app, where is the file?

36:31.820 --> 36:33.460
It doesn't exist in a folder I can see.

36:33.600 --> 36:36.080
It is trapped in the app's proprietary database.

36:36.300 --> 36:36.560
Mm-hmm.

36:36.780 --> 36:38.440
I can't just drag it onto my desktop.

36:38.660 --> 36:44.700
I have to hit a share button, navigate a menu, and hope the app allows me to export it, usually at a lower resolution.

36:45.700 --> 36:47.880
Flyxion uses a mathematical term for this too, right?

36:48.360 --> 36:49.640
Non-faithful functors.

36:49.640 --> 36:50.900
Yes, non-faithful functors.

36:51.060 --> 36:52.640
A functor is a mapping between categories.

36:53.440 --> 36:53.640
Okay.

36:53.720 --> 36:59.060
A faithful functor maps things in a way that preserves all the distinct structures and relationships of the original data.

36:59.360 --> 37:00.620
You can see how everything connects.

37:00.880 --> 37:01.760
Like the old folders.

37:01.940 --> 37:02.360
Exactly.

37:02.860 --> 37:05.720
A non-faithful functor collapses those distinctions.

37:06.260 --> 37:12.740
It takes rich, structured data and squashes it flat, hiding the underlying mechanics behind a simplified interface.

37:12.980 --> 37:14.180
Which ruins everything.

37:14.180 --> 37:19.080
When an app does this, it destroys what software engineers call composability.

37:19.760 --> 37:26.080
Composability being the ability to take the output of one tool and smoothly use it as the raw input for another tool.

37:26.180 --> 37:26.380
Right.

37:26.660 --> 37:28.160
Like clicking Lego bricks together.

37:28.360 --> 37:28.600
Yes.

37:29.440 --> 37:31.600
Composability is what gives the user power.

37:31.920 --> 37:37.440
But modern tech monopolies recognized a fundamental truth of political economy.

37:37.720 --> 37:38.300
Which is?

37:38.340 --> 37:41.040
If users have composability, users have freedom.

37:41.200 --> 37:41.720
Exactly.

37:41.720 --> 37:46.460
If your data is portable, you can easily leave their ecosystem and use a competitor's tool.

37:46.960 --> 37:50.720
So it is in the financial interest of the platform to deliberately restrict composability.

37:51.480 --> 37:51.580
Right.

37:51.620 --> 37:53.200
They trap your data in their silos.

37:53.440 --> 37:56.220
They replace raw files with non-faithful interfaces.

37:56.500 --> 37:56.600
Yep.

37:56.860 --> 38:05.640
In doing so, they strip away your epistemic agency, your ability to view the true state of your own information, enforce your own constraints, and build your own workflows.

38:06.140 --> 38:08.780
It is exactly what the AI databases are doing.

38:08.780 --> 38:18.680
The tech platforms are hoarding the data, hiding the real state of the world, tolerating inconsistencies, and preventing us from reaching closure so we stay dependent on them.

38:18.780 --> 38:19.160
Yes.

38:19.160 --> 38:27.340
The architecture of a walled garden smartphone app is structurally identical to the architecture of a hallucinating large language model.

38:27.780 --> 38:29.460
It all comes back to the core thesis.

38:30.120 --> 38:46.420
Whether you are looking at a neural network failing to reason, a tech platform trapping your data, an algorithmic bureaucracy executing administrative violence, or a jawless fish navigating the ocean, the fundamental reality of the system is defined by its hidden constraints.

38:46.420 --> 38:48.440
Let's pull all the threads together then.

38:48.760 --> 38:55.080
We started this deep drive looking for the grand unified theory connecting Flyxion's work across biology, political science, and mathematics.

38:55.320 --> 38:55.980
And we found it.

38:56.240 --> 38:57.640
It is overwhelmingly clear.

38:58.220 --> 39:03.920
True power, true form, and true reality do not lie in the visible traits or the spectacular events.

39:04.280 --> 39:05.940
They lie in the invisible architecture.

39:05.940 --> 39:11.700
In biology, the continuous tensile field of collagen dictates what a physical body can be.

39:12.320 --> 39:14.620
It shapes the undulatory swimming of the lamprey.

39:15.240 --> 39:26.180
And when ecological constraints reach the absolute limit of the deep sea, it forces the immune systems of anglerfish to surrender individuality and physically fuse two bodies into one just to survive.

39:26.180 --> 39:31.140
In human society, bureaucratic algorithms act as the invisible constraint field.

39:31.600 --> 39:34.560
They dictate who thrives and who is starved of resources.

39:34.860 --> 39:34.940
Right.

39:35.260 --> 39:46.680
They execute a quiet, devastating administrative violence, delegating the locus of decision logic to black box math, while we are endlessly distracted by the cinematic spectacle of killer drones.

39:46.680 --> 39:50.320
And in computation, hidden mathematical states dictate everything.

39:50.880 --> 39:56.920
Without constraint closure, our AI systems will just accumulate data and confidently hallucinate impossibilities.

39:57.040 --> 39:57.320
Exactly.

39:57.540 --> 40:06.380
And without access to the underlying state of our own data, we are trapped in corporate software silos, completely stripped of our ability to compose our own digital lives.

40:06.440 --> 40:08.620
It is all about navigating the hidden gradients.

40:08.980 --> 40:09.620
It really is.

40:09.700 --> 40:09.900
Yeah.

40:09.900 --> 40:16.880
Which leaves me with a final, slightly terrifying, but I think incredibly necessary thought for you to chew on after this.

40:16.920 --> 40:17.540
Let's hear it.

40:17.780 --> 40:32.320
If biological organisms, human societies, and artificial intelligences are all essentially just entities navigating invisible gradient fields created by hidden constraints, what does that say about our own free will?

40:32.460 --> 40:33.140
Oh, wow.

40:33.140 --> 40:40.620
Think about the choices you are going to make today, the things you desire, the career path you are stressing over, the articles you click on, the way you spend your money.

40:40.940 --> 40:43.460
Are those choices truly your own?

40:43.620 --> 40:43.880
Right.

40:44.060 --> 40:47.420
Are they the product of an independent, unconstrained mind?

40:47.640 --> 40:47.880
Right.

40:48.000 --> 40:50.980
Or are you simply a nematode following a chemical trail?

40:50.980 --> 41:00.340
Are you just navigating an invisible algorithmic gradient, a constraint space, that's someone or something else designed to guide you exactly where they want you to go?

41:00.520 --> 41:02.580
Are we the player in the video game making the choices?

41:02.840 --> 41:06.760
Or are we simply the physics engine blindly executing the code we were given?

41:07.100 --> 41:09.120
It changes how you look at everything.

41:09.120 --> 41:21.900
Next time you feel that flash of anger because an app won't let you move a simple file, or the next time you read a terrifying headline about the future of AI, remember the Roman battlefields of Cinné.

41:22.540 --> 41:24.340
Remember the deep sea anglerfish.

41:24.600 --> 41:25.560
Don't look at the spectacle.

41:26.320 --> 41:27.620
Look for the invisible constraints.

41:28.140 --> 41:29.120
Words to live by.

41:29.400 --> 41:31.520
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

41:31.780 --> 41:32.820
We'll see you in the gradients.

